// Formative practice quiz for usestate. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/components-and-state/usestate

::usestate o1 - handler form::Which onClick value correctly runs a handler named save only when the button is clicked?{
=The function itself, onClick set to save with no parentheses#Correct. You pass the function and React calls it on click.
~save followed by parentheses, inside the braces#Not quite. That calls save while rendering and passes its return value instead.
~The string save#Not quite. React expects a function, not a name in a string.
~Nothing, since React finds save by name#Not quite. React does not look up handlers by name.
}

::usestate o1 - useState returns::What does a call to useState with a starting value return?{
~Only the current value#Not quite. Without a setter you could not change the value.
~An object with get and set methods#Not quite. useState returns an array, not an object with methods.
=A two-item array\: the current value and a setter function#Correct. That is why it is usually written with array destructuring.
~A promise for the value#Not quite. State is available during render without awaiting.
}

::usestate o2 - local variable::A component changes a let variable in a click handler and the screen does not update. What is the reason?{
~let variables are read-only inside handlers#Not quite. let can be reassigned. The variable does change; the screen simply is not redrawn.
=Changing a local variable does not trigger a render#Correct. Only state setters ask React to render again.
~The handler never runs#Not quite. It runs, and the variable changes. Nothing re-renders.
~React only displays strings#Not quite. React displays numbers too.
}

::usestate o3 - same snapshot::From n equal to 0, one click handler calls setN with n plus 1 three times. What shows after the next render?{
~3#Not quite. Three calls would make 3 only with updater functions.
~0#Not quite. The setters do request an update, so the value does change.
~2#Not quite. Every call reads the same n, and so sets the same value.
=1#Correct. n is 0 for that whole handler, so each call sets 1.
}

::usestate o3 - updater when needed::When should you pass an updater function to a state setter?{
~Whenever the state is a string#Not quite. The type of the state does not matter here.
~Only inside Effects#Not quite. Updaters work anywhere you call a setter.
~Always, because plain values are not allowed#Not quite. Plain values are fine when the next value does not depend on the previous one.
=When the next value is computed from the previous value#Correct. An updater receives the latest queued value, not the snapshot.
}
